# Sorting algorithms

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Space | Stable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| insertion | n | n² | n² | 1 | yes |
| merge | n log n | n log n | n log n | n | yes |
| quick | n log n | n log n | n² | log n | no |
| heap | n log n | n log n | n log n | 1 | no |
| counting | n + k | n + k | n + k | k | yes |
| radix (LSD) | d(n + k) | d(n + k) | d(n + k) | n + k | yes |

Comparison sorts can't beat Ω(n log n) in the worst case (decision tree has n! leaves, height ≥ log2 n!).

Quicksort pivot: median of three, or random. Introsort (C++ std::sort) switches to heapsort if recursion gets too deep, and to insertion sort for tiny ranges.

Timsort (Python, Java objects): finds natural runs, merges them with galloping. Excellent on partially sorted data.

Stability matters when you sort by one key and then another (sort by name, then stable sort by grade).
